 ## Shared principles
 All methods in this family inherit the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting representation and rendering pipeline from [Scene Representation and Geometry Constraints](15421-scene-representation-and-geometry-constraints.md) [G]. The core variation across methods lies in the definition, storage, and optimization of $\mathbf{c}_i(\omega)$ and potentially $\alpha_i$, moving beyond the fixed SH coefficients used in base 3DGS [G]. Common strategies include:
-1.  **Post-Processing Transformation:** Applying a learned affine transformation to the rendered image based on 3D position and view embedding [Color and Appearance Modeling](14740-color-and-appearance-modeling.md).
-2.  **Factorized Illumination:** Separating scene lighting (via a tensor grid) from material properties (via BRDF features) to model specular effects [Color and Appearance Modeling](14740-color-and-appearance-modeling.md).
-3.  **Image-Conditioned Modulation:** Using an MLP and per-image embeddings to modulate Gaussian color and opacity, enabling transient object removal and appearance synthesis [Color and Appearance Modeling](14740-color-and-appearance-modeling.md).
-4.  **Neural Field Substitution:** Replacing per-Gaussian SH with a shared grid-based neural field to reduce parameters and improve generalization [Color and Appearance Modeling](14740-color-and-appearance-modeling.md).
-5.  **Volumetric Attenuation:** Modifying the alpha-blending equation to incorporate medium properties, such as direct attenuation $B_d$ and backscatter parameters $B_\infty, B_b$, to model light transport in non-vacuum media [Physics and Optical Modeling](15148-physics-and-optical-modeling.md).
+1.  **Post-Processing Transformation:** Applying a learned affine transformation to the rendered image based on 3D position and view embedding [G].
+2.  **Factorized Illumination:** Separating scene lighting (via a tensor grid) from material properties (via BRDF features) to model specular effects [G].
+3.  **Image-Conditioned Modulation:** Using an MLP and per-image embeddings to modulate Gaussian color and opacity, enabling transient object removal and appearance synthesis [G].
+4.  **Neural Field Substitution:** Replacing per-Gaussian SH with a shared grid-based neural field to reduce parameters and improve generalization [G].
+5.  **Volumetric Attenuation:** Modifying the alpha-blending equation to incorporate medium properties, such as direct attenuation $B_d$ and backscatter parameters $B_\infty, B_b$, to model light transport in non-vacuum media [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Color and Appearance Modeling](14740-color-and-appearance-modeling.md)
-**What it is:** Extends the standard 3DGS representation by replacing or augmenting the fixed Spherical Harmonic (SH) coefficients with more flexible mechanisms to represent view-dependent color, lighting, and material properties.
-**What sets it apart:** Focuses on radiance and material representation rather than volumetric light transport equations.
-**When to choose:** When base 3DGS struggles with complex specularities, dynamic lighting variations, or transient objects in unstructured datasets.
